course: dictionary
lesson: 1895
title: Colonisation in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Colonisation means one country takes control of another land and sends its people to live there, often taking power from the people who already live there. You can work this out from the context. «British colonisation from 1788 brought disease, violence and the taking of their land.» The clues are disease, violence and taking land. These are harms, so colonisation is not a friendly word.
explain: Look at who was there first. «Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived in Australia for at least 50,000 years.» If those peoples lived there for thousands of years, then colonisation must be the arrival of new people who took control. Check it by swapping in your meaning: «British people came, took control and sent people to live there» still makes sense.
explain: In science, the same word has a different meaning. «Plants colonise bare ground after a fire» means that new plants move into the empty ground and start living there. The clues are bare ground and after a fire. So always check which subject you are reading, because the meaning can change.
explain: You may also see colonization with a z. This is the American spelling of the same word, and it has the same meaning. When a word has a hard history, like this one, a good dictionary helps you check the facts and the meaning.
fact: Colonisation means one country takes control of another land and sends people to live there.
fact: In science, colonisation can mean plants or animals move into a new place and start living there.
fact: «Indigenous peoples» means the first peoples who lived in a place, such as the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples of Australia.
fact: «British colonisation from 1788 brought disease, violence and the taking of their land.»
fact: Many Aboriginal children were taken from their families; they are called the Stolen Generations.
exercise: «British colonisation from 1788 brought disease, violence and the taking of their land.» What does colonisation mean here? | A friendly trade between two villages | *One country taking control of another land and sending people to live there | A big party to welcome visitors | Plants moving into bare ground after a fire
exercise: In science, «Plants colonise bare ground after a fire.» What does colonise mean? | *Plants move into a new place and start living there | Plants are burnt to ash by the fire | Plants are picked and taken home | Plants stop growing because it is cold
exercise: «Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived in Australia for at least 50,000 years.» Who were there first? | *Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples | Plants on bare ground | British colonisers arriving in 1788 | Explorers who came by ship
test: In history, «the colonisation of Australia» means | a school trip to a museum | *one country taking control of another land and sending people to live there | a famous battle won in a single day | a new town built by its own people
test: Which clue in «British colonisation brought disease, violence and the taking of their land» shows that it was harmful? | *disease, violence and the taking of their land | colonisation | British | 1788
exam: Which sentence uses colonisation in the science sense? | «The British colonised Australia in 1788.» | «Aboriginal peoples have lived in Australia for 50,000 years.» | «The settlers built a fort by the river.» | *«Seeds colonise the bare hillside after the fire.»
